WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.690 --> 00:00:06.310
So we’re in the basement of the Royal Society
in London. This is the UK

00:00:06.310 --> 00:00:10.459
Academy of Sciences. They have exhibitions
here telling people about

00:00:10.459 --> 00:00:13.759
interesting things about science, and so we
have come here because

00:00:13.759 --> 00:00:19.230
there’s a really interesting exhibit relevant
to the work of Lawrence who

00:00:19.230 --> 00:00:24.540
was the person after whom the element Lawrencium
was named after.

00:00:24.540 --> 00:00:29.200
Lawrencium is another one of the elements
right at the bottom of the

00:00:29.200 --> 00:00:34.079
periodic table which are really heavy, very
radioactive. They fall to

00:00:34.079 --> 00:00:39.449
pieces very rapidly. So chemists are very,
and physicists are very lucky

00:00:39.449 --> 00:00:43.769
if they just make a few atoms of this. So
there’s nothing to tell you

00:00:43.769 --> 00:00:47.730
about the uses of lawrencium, because nobody’s
made enough

00:00:47.730 --> 00:00:51.839
lawrencium to use it for anything, except
as the subject of scientific

00:00:51.839 --> 00:00:56.659
papers and physical measurements. So instead
I want to show you here

00:00:56.659 --> 00:01:00.569
a piece of equipment which is very similar
to the sort of equipment that

00:01:00.569 --> 00:01:04.290
Lawrence used when he was doing his pioneering
work in Berkeley,

00:01:04.290 --> 00:01:08.710
California, close to San Francisco. So let’s
go along here.

00:01:08.710 --> 00:01:17.470
Lawrence was famous for inventing an instrument
called the Cyclotron

00:01:17.470 --> 00:01:22.930
which was for accelerating electrons to very
high speeds. It was in fact

00:01:22.930 --> 00:01:27.200
the precursor of the large Hadron Collider
which everybody’s now talking

00:01:27.200 --> 00:01:33.420
about. But he thought of it I think one day
in the library in Berkeley, and

00:01:33.420 --> 00:01:37.530
he imagined to begin with an instrument that
was literally as big as my

00:01:37.530 --> 00:01:43.680
two hands here, a few inches across, and his
idea was that if you made

00:01:43.680 --> 00:01:50.600
a vessel which was quite flat and circular
not very high and put in it a big

00:01:50.600 --> 00:01:55.960
magnetic field the electrons could go round
and round and round, and be

00:01:55.960 --> 00:02:00.180
accelerated to go faster and faster. The idea
was that he made one and

00:02:00.180 --> 00:02:05.060
demonstrated the principle and he patented
it and then he built bigger

00:02:05.060 --> 00:02:09.160
and bigger and bigger machines and as he made
them, not only did the

00:02:09.160 --> 00:02:14.730
disc get bigger, but at the same time the
magnet got bigger as well. And

00:02:14.730 --> 00:02:20.690
the final magnet that he made in Berkeley,
which was installed just

00:02:20.690 --> 00:02:25.530
before the Second World War, was so large
that a large part of his

00:02:25.530 --> 00:02:29.451
research group could sit between the poles
of the magnet and it’s so

00:02:29.451 --> 00:02:36.470
heavy that the magnet is still there to this
day. But come and look over

00:02:36.470 --> 00:02:43.810
here. This is a Cyclotron that was built in
Cambridge. The first thing that

00:02:43.810 --> 00:02:49.500
strikes you is that it doesn’t look very
exciting. It’s two copper discs,

00:02:49.500 --> 00:02:56.350
which are not actually connected together
and the voltage is switched

00:02:56.350 --> 00:03:01.270
between one disc and the other so as the electron
goes round when it

00:03:01.270 --> 00:03:04.560
gets to the end of one you switch the voltage
so it goes on being

00:03:04.560 --> 00:03:10.960
accelerated and it goes round and round, and
as it goes round in a

00:03:10.960 --> 00:03:17.630
magnetic field the faster it goes the bigger
the circle. So it goes round

00:03:17.630 --> 00:03:22.099
and round and round in a larger and larger
circle until the end it flies out

00:03:22.099 --> 00:03:28.599
of one of these tubes. I think again that
it is important to say that some

00:03:28.599 --> 00:03:33.270
of these historic physics experiments, which
are so important to both

00:03:33.270 --> 00:03:38.040
chemistry and physics, were done with equipment
that really looks

00:03:38.040 --> 00:03:43.730
surprisingly simple and it is the really clever
ideas that have been so

00:03:43.730 --> 00:03:47.819
important to physics and chemistry rather
than always having

00:03:47.819 --> 00:03:53.700
enormously complicated apparatus. Sometimes
to take these ideas to

00:03:53.700 --> 00:03:57.980
their full extreme you do need complicated
apparatus but that’s not the

00:03:57.980 --> 00:03:59.250
starting point.

00:03:59.250 --> 00:04:02.910
Well I’ve read quite a bit about him. He
was meant to be a very

00:04:02.910 --> 00:04:11.790
egotistical person and a real control freak,
but my father-in-law worked

00:04:11.790 --> 00:04:15.880
in Lawrence’s research group. When he was
an English undergraduate he

00:04:15.880 --> 00:04:21.850
was sent to America during the Second World
War to work in Lawrence’s

00:04:21.850 --> 00:04:28.010
group on the enrichment of uranium isotopes.
That is, concentrating

00:04:28.010 --> 00:04:34.840
Uranium-235, the less-common isotope which
was the basis for the atom

00:04:34.840 --> 00:04:40.660
bomb that was dropped on Hiroshima.

00:04:40.660 --> 00:04:46.990
So John Keane, my father-in-law, worked in
the research group, I don’t

00:04:46.990 --> 00:04:51.910
know exactly what he did, but it’s really
quite a sobering thought that

00:04:51.910 --> 00:04:56.740
during the Second World War some of the brightest
scientists had to

00:04:56.740 --> 00:05:06.440
work on these terrible projects really in
order to save their countries.

00:05:06.440 --> 00:05:09.060
And let us hope that these such things never
happen again.

